1
00:00:00,860 --> 00:00:04,660
When something breaks, the
first question is always:

2
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:09,240
what happened, and in what order?
The answers are in your logs,

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:15,100
spread over every host. OpenChronology
turns each log entry into a moment on one

4
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:16,517
timeline.

5
00:00:18,017 --> 00:00:22,157
For logs you already have,
there's a command-line importer.

6
00:00:22,207 --> 00:00:26,497
Point it at syslog files,
Apache or nginx logs,

7
00:00:26,547 --> 00:00:31,967
JSON lines or the journal, gzipped or
not, and try it with a dry run first.

8
00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:35,357
Each entry keeps its fields
as tags and metadata,

9
00:00:35,407 --> 00:00:37,779
and its severity.

10
00:00:39,249 --> 00:00:45,439
Or stream them as they happen. A syslog
receiver listens on UDP and TCP,

11
00:00:45,489 --> 00:00:48,199
and a journal follower
reads the systemd journal,

12
00:00:48,249 --> 00:00:53,679
here or over SSH. Routing rules send
each entry to the right timeline,

13
00:00:53,729 --> 00:00:57,589
and anyone with it open watches
the new entries arrive.

14
00:00:59,039 --> 00:01:03,509
Here's one night's incident: three
hours of syslog from four hosts,

15
00:01:03,559 --> 00:01:08,549
about two thousand three hundred entries.
From a distance they're summaries.

16
00:01:08,599 --> 00:01:11,949
Zoom into the outage, and
the colours tell the story:

17
00:01:11,999 --> 00:01:15,269
a run of red five-oh-twos
from both web servers,

18
00:01:15,319 --> 00:01:19,176
and just before them, critical
errors from the database.

19
00:01:20,596 --> 00:01:26,496
Separate by tags, choose the database
host, and its entries get a track of their

20
00:01:26,546 --> 00:01:31,066
own: disk usage climbing for forty
minutes, then no space left,

21
00:01:31,116 --> 00:01:36,656
and PostgreSQL stops. Go back to the
start of the climb and there's a backup

22
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:41,816
job, writing to the database's own disk.
Add what you found as moments of your

23
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:46,243
own, link cause to effect, and the
timeline becomes the write-up.

24
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:52,983
Every entry keeps its exact time, to
the microsecond where the log has it,

25
00:01:53,033 --> 00:01:58,213
so entries from different hosts sort
correctly. A timeline that keeps growing

26
00:01:58,263 --> 00:02:02,753
can keep only its recent past,
such as the last ninety days.

27
00:02:02,803 --> 00:02:07,052
And one timeline holds up to
two hundred thousand entries.

28
00:02:08,512 --> 00:02:12,052
Point your syslog at it, and
the next time something breaks,

29
00:02:12,102 --> 00:02:14,355
the timeline is already there.
